The stack keeps indices of bars in increasing height order. When a shorter bar appears, the bars popped from the stack cannot extend any farther right. The new stack top gives their left limit, so the area can be calculated immediately.

A virtual bar of height 0 is processed at the end. This flushes bars that reach the right edge without changing the input array.

```java
class Solution {
    public int largestRectangleArea(int[] heights) {
        int n = heights.length;
        java.util.ArrayDeque<Integer> stack = new java.util.ArrayDeque<>();
        int best = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i <= n; ++i) {
            int currentHeight = (i == n) ? 0 : heights[i];

            while (!stack.isEmpty() && heights[stack.peek()] >= currentHeight) {
                int barIndex = stack.pop();
                int leftBoundary = stack.isEmpty() ? -1 : stack.peek();
                int width = i - leftBoundary - 1;
                long area = (long) heights[barIndex] * width;
                best = Math.max(best, (int) area);
            }

            if (i < n) {
                stack.push(i);
            }
        }

        return best;
    }
}
```

In the sample `[2, 1, 5, 6, 2, 3]`, the bar of height 2 at index 4 pops 6 first, then 5. When 5 is popped, the left boundary is index 1 and the current index 4 is the right boundary, giving width 2 and area 10.

The code does not modify `heights`. The cast to `long` makes the multiplication safe before the result is stored as the required integer.

Time complexity: `O(n)`. Space complexity: `O(n)` in the increasing-height case.

Instead of storing only an index, this stack stores a height and the first position where that height can start. When a taller bar is removed, its width is added to the shorter bar that replaces it. This avoids calculating separate left and right arrays.

A zero-height value at the end makes all remaining bars get processed.

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <utility>
#include <algorithm>
using namespace std;

class Solution
{
public:
    int largestRectangleArea(vector<int> &heights) {
        vector<pair<int, int>> stack;
        long long best = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i <= static_cast<int>(heights.size()); ++i) {
            int currentHeight = (i == static_cast<int>(heights.size()))
                    ? 0 : heights[i];
            int start = i;

            while (!stack.empty() && stack.back().first >= currentHeight) {
                int height = stack.back().first;
                start = stack.back().second;
                stack.pop_back();

                long long width = i - start;
                best = max(best, width * height);
            }

            if (stack.empty() || stack.back().first < currentHeight) {
                stack.push_back({currentHeight, start});
            }
        }

        return static_cast<int>(best);
    }
};
```

For `[2, 4, 2]`, the height 4 bar starts at position 1. When the final zero is reached, it contributes area 4. The height 2 entry has start position 0 and spans all three bars, giving area 6.

An empty input goes straight through the loop with the virtual zero and returns 0. The input vector is not changed.

Every stack entry is pushed and removed at most once, so the running time is `O(n)`. The stack can hold all bars, which uses `O(n)` extra space.

For each bar, the useful information is the first shorter bar on both sides. The left boundary comes from a scan from left to right, and the right boundary comes from scanning in the other direction.

The stacks store indices. Bars that are at least as tall as the current bar are removed, so the remaining top is the closest smaller boundary.

```python
class Solution:
    def largestRectangleArea(self, heights):
        n = len(heights)
        if n == 0:
            return 0

        left_smaller = [-1] * n
        right_smaller = [n] * n

        stack = []
        for i in range(n):
            while stack and heights[stack[-1]] >= heights[i]:
                stack.pop()
            if stack:
                left_smaller[i] = stack[-1]
            stack.append(i)

        stack = []
        for i in range(n - 1, -1, -1):
            while stack and heights[stack[-1]] >= heights[i]:
                stack.pop()
            if stack:
                right_smaller[i] = stack[-1]
            stack.append(i)

        largest = 0
        for i in range(n):
            width = right_smaller[i] - left_smaller[i] - 1
            largest = max(largest, heights[i] * width)

        return largest
```

Dry run for `[2, 1, 5, 6, 2, 3]`: bar 5 at index 2 has left boundary 1 and right boundary 4. Its width is `4 - 1 - 1 = 2`, so its area is `5 * 2 = 10`.

The empty list is handled before indexing. Equal heights are popped too, which keeps the boundary arrays simple.

Time complexity: `O(n)`, since every index enters and leaves each stack once. Space complexity: `O(n)` for the two boundary arrays and the stacks.

A direct version is useful for checking the idea. For every starting bar, extend the range to the right and keep the smallest height seen so far. That smallest height is the rectangle height for the current range.

This is easy to follow, but it will be slow for a large histogram.

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>
using namespace std;

class Solution
{
public:
    int largestRectangleArea(vector<int> &heights) {
        int n = heights.size();
        long long best = 0;

        for (int left = 0; left < n; ++left) {
            int shortest = heights[left];

            for (int right = left; right < n; ++right) {
                shortest = min(shortest, heights[right]);
                long long width = right - left + 1;
                best = max(best, width * shortest);
            }
        }

        return static_cast<int>(best);
    }
};
```

For `[2, 1, 5, 6, 2, 3]`, the range from index 2 to index 3 has minimum height 5 and width 2, giving area 10.

Time complexity: `O(n^2)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space.

This version uses divide and conquer. For any range, find its shortest bar. A rectangle covering the whole range uses that bar as its height. The answer must therefore be the best of the whole range, the left part, and the right part.

A segment tree keeps the index of the shortest bar in every range, so each minimum query is quick. It is more code than a stack, but it gives a different way to see the problem.

```java
class Solution {
    private int[] heights;
    private int[] tree;

    public int largestRectangleArea(int[] heights) {
        if (heights.length == 0) {
            return 0;
        }

        this.heights = heights;
        tree = new int[heights.length * 4];
        build(1, 0, heights.length - 1);

        return findBest(0, heights.length - 1);
    }

    private void build(int node, int left, int right) {
        if (left == right) {
            tree[node] = left;
            return;
        }

        int middle = left + (right - left) / 2;
        build(node * 2, left, middle);
        build(node * 2 + 1, middle + 1, right);

        int leftIndex = tree[node * 2];
        int rightIndex = tree[node * 2 + 1];
        tree[node] = heights[leftIndex] <= heights[rightIndex]
                ? leftIndex : rightIndex;
    }

    private int findMinimumIndex(int node, int start, int end,
                                 int queryLeft, int queryRight) {
        if (queryRight < start || end < queryLeft) {
            return -1;
        }

        if (queryLeft <= start && end <= queryRight) {
            return tree[node];
        }

        int middle = start + (end - start) / 2;
        int leftIndex = findMinimumIndex(node * 2, start, middle,
                queryLeft, queryRight);
        int rightIndex = findMinimumIndex(node * 2 + 1, middle + 1, end,
                queryLeft, queryRight);

        if (leftIndex == -1) {
            return rightIndex;
        }
        if (rightIndex == -1) {
            return leftIndex;
        }

        return heights[leftIndex] <= heights[rightIndex]
                ? leftIndex : rightIndex;
    }

    private int findBest(int left, int right) {
        if (left > right) {
            return 0;
        }

        int minimumIndex = findMinimumIndex(1, 0, heights.length - 1,
                left, right);
        long longFix = (long) heights[minimumIndex] * (right - left + 1);

        int leftBest = findBest(left, minimumIndex - 1);
        int rightBest = findBest(minimumIndex + 1, right);

        return (int) Math.max(longFix, Math.max(leftBest, rightBest));
    }
}
```

For an empty array, the method returns 0 before building the tree. With a single bar, the selected range has width 1.

Building the tree takes `O(n)`. The recursive range processing takes `O(n log n)` in the usual balanced case. Space complexity is `O(n)` for the tree and recursion.

## Solution
```java
class Solution {
    public int largestRectangleArea(int[] heights) {
        LinkedList<Integer> stack = new LinkedList<>();
        int max=Integer.MIN_VALUE;
        for(int i=0; i<=heights.length; i++){
            while(!stack.isEmpty() && (i==heights.length || heights[stack.peek()] > heights[i])){
                int current = stack.pop();

                int width=0;
                if(stack.isEmpty()){
                    width = i;
                }else{
                    width = i-stack.peek()-1;
                }
                max = Math.max(max, width*heights[current]);
            }
            stack.push(i);
        }
        return max;
    }
}

```

